# `!has_error` is `!` and the name `has_error`, not the operator `!has` (LANGUAGE.md section 1): DS0111 with a fix to `not(has_error)`.
mode: syntax
---- query
'events.csv' | where !has_error
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..32
  PIPELINE@0..31
    LITERAL@0..12
      STRING@0..12 "'events.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@12..13 " "
    PIPE@13..14 "|"
    WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
    WHERE_OP@15..31
      IDENT@15..20 "where"
      WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
      PREFIX_EXPR@21..31
        BANG@21..22 "!"
        NAME_REF@22..31
          IDENT@22..31 "has_error"
  WHITESPACE@31..32 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:22
  |
1 | 'events.csv' | where !has_error
  |                      ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(has_error)`
  = note: `!has_error` is `!` followed by the name `has_error`; the negated operator `!has` goes between two values: `a !has b`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    'events.csv' | where not(has_error)
which parses without errors
